WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.288
Welcome to Grade 3 English, Unit 4: Places and Community.

2
00:00:04.368 --> 00:00:11.416
In this unit you will explore the places in
your community and the people who help you there.

3
00:00:11.496 --> 00:00:19.003
You will learn to name places like the hospital,
the market, the court, the college and the garden,

4
00:00:19.083 --> 00:00:24.367
and to talk about helpers like the
doctor, the officer and the sailor.

5
00:00:24.447 --> 00:00:33.104
You will describe places using words like quiet and busy,
give clear instructions, ask questions with do and does,

6
00:00:33.184 --> 00:00:36.706
and explain your ideas with the word “because”.

7
00:00:36.786 --> 00:00:43.450
By the end, you will be ready to write your
own report about a place in your community —

8
00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:46.592
just like Amal does in this unit's story.

9
00:00:46.672 --> 00:00:53.030
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

10
00:00:53.110 --> 00:01:00.988
By the end of this unit, you will be able to name
places in your community and the people who work there.

11
00:01:01.068 --> 00:01:07.278
You will also describe a place using
adjectives and opposites, like quiet and busy.

12
00:01:07.358 --> 00:01:13.644
You will also give simple instructions
using imperatives (“Exit the court quietly.”)

13
00:01:13.724 --> 00:01:16.448
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

14
00:01:16.528 --> 00:01:22.638
Your vocabulary work begins with these words:
doctor, officer, sailor, hospital, court, college.

15
00:01:22.718 --> 00:01:27.860
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

16
00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:31.921
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

17
00:01:32.001 --> 00:01:36.438
The reading sequence includes Our
Community and A Poem: “Places I Know”.

18
00:01:36.518 --> 00:01:37.316
Read actively.

19
00:01:37.396 --> 00:01:41.644
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

20
00:01:41.724 --> 00:01:45.722
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

21
00:01:45.802 --> 00:01:50.118
The first language focus is Imperatives (Giving Instructions).

22
00:01:50.198 --> 00:01:55.862
An imperative is a special way to use a
verb to give a command or an instruction.

23
00:01:55.942 --> 00:01:58.203
It tells someone to do something.

24
00:01:58.283 --> 00:02:06.429
We need imperatives to give clear directions, like “Exit
the court quietly” or “Go to the hospital if you are sick.”

25
00:02:06.509 --> 00:02:14.158
Notice the structure in the model examples, then apply
it deliberately in connected sentences and paragraphs.

26
00:02:14.238 --> 00:02:18.027
In speaking, you will complete Speaking 1 — My Community.

27
00:02:18.107 --> 00:02:22.573
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

28
00:02:22.653 --> 00:02:26.035
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

29
00:02:26.115 --> 00:02:29.631
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

30
00:02:29.711 --> 00:02:31.274
You are ready to begin.

31
00:02:31.354 --> 00:02:38.346
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

32
00:02:38.426 --> 00:02:41.775
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

33
00:02:41.855 --> 00:02:49.704
Use AI English for hints and feedback any time, and bring
your questions to a live session too if you join one.
